/**
 * @file spl_authoring.dox
 * @brief Tutorial for writing complete SPL specifications in the repository DSL.
 *
 * @page spl_authoring_guide How to write a complete SPL file
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_start Start here
 *
 * This page is the user-facing authoring guide for the repository DSL. It is
 * intended to be sufficient for writing a complete valid SPL file without
 * opening the Markdown files under `external_documentation/`.
 *
 * For the parser and executable semantics behind this guide, see
 * @ref spl_parser. For canonical examples and common mistakes, see
 * @ref spl_examples_guide.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_nav_sec Navigation and related pages
 *
 * Recommended reading order:
 *
 * - @ref documentation_guide
 * - @ref spl_authoring_guide
 * - @ref spl_examples_guide
 * - @ref spl_parser
 *
 * Related pages:
 *
 * - @ref feature_analysis

 * @section spl_authoring_scope What the DSL describes
 *
 * A repository SPL file declares:
 *
 * - the Boolean **fundamental features** of the product line,
 * - optional logical constraints over those features,
 * - optional information about which valid systems are available or missing,
 * - an optional reporting filter over derived-feature categories.
 *
 * The DSL does not list derived features directly. The implementation derives
 * them after it computes the valid system space.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_lexical Lexical rules
 *
 * The current tokenizer accepts the following conventions:
 *
 * - whitespace between tokens is ignored,
 * - `//` starts a line comment that runs to the end of the line,
 * - names are word tokens built from letters, digits, `_`, `#`, and `.`,
 * - integer literals are non-negative decimal integers,
 * - word keywords are case-insensitive.
 *
 * The symbolic operators accepted by the current parser are:
 *
 * - `!`
 * - `&&`
 * - `||`
 * - `->`
 * - `<->`
 * - `(`
 * - `)`
 * - `[`
 * - `]`
 * - `,`
 * - `=`
 * - `!=`
 * - `<`
 * - `<=`
 * - `>`
 * - `>=`
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_skeleton Full file skeleton
 *
 * The intended top-level shape of a complete SPL file is:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * [ SPL <name> ]
 * FEATURES <count> <feature-1> ... <feature-n>
 * [ CONSTRAINTS
 *     ASSERT <formula>
 *     ...
 *   END ]
 * [ AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS <system-id> ... [END] ]
 * [ MISSING_SYSTEMS <system-id> ... [END] ]
 * [ CATEGORY_FILTER <category> ... [END] ]
 * @endcode
 *
 * `FEATURES` is mandatory. All other sections are optional.
 *
 * The grammar intends each top-level section to appear at most once. The
 * current parser does not reject repeated sections explicitly; later parsed
 * payload can overwrite earlier payload. Do not rely on that implementation
 * detail.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_sections Section-by-section guide
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_spl SPL
 *
 * `SPL` stores an optional product-line name.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL FactorialPaper
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_features FEATURES
 *
 * `FEATURES` declares the Boolean fundamental-feature basis.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * FEATURES 3 fa fb fc
 * @endcode
 *
 * The first number is the declared number of feature names that must follow.
 * Feature names used later in formulas must be declared here first.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_constraints CONSTRAINTS
 *
 * `CONSTRAINTS` is optional. It contains zero or more `ASSERT` statements and
 * is terminated by `END`.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT fa -> fb
 *   ASSERT !(fb && fc)
 * END
 * @endcode
 *
 * All asserted formulas are conjoined. If the block is omitted, there are no
 * constraints and every candidate system in `P(F)` is valid.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_available AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS
 *
 * `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS` lists valid systems that are available to the repository
 * for analysis.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS 1 3 4 END
 * @endcode
 *
 * The parser accepts `END`, but it also stops this section at the next
 * top-level section or at end-of-file. Using `END` is still the clearest style.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_missing MISSING_SYSTEMS
 *
 * `MISSING_SYSTEMS` lists valid systems that are known to be missing.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * MISSING_SYSTEMS 1 END
 * @endcode
 *
 * The same stop rules apply as for `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS`.
 *
 * If only one of `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS` or `MISSING_SYSTEMS` is present, the other
 * side is derived by complement within the valid system space. If neither is
 * present, all valid systems are treated as available.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_filter CATEGORY_FILTER
 *
 * `CATEGORY_FILTER` is an optional reporting filter. It does not change
 * parsing, constraint evaluation, validity, or derived-feature construction.
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * CATEGORY_FILTER Core Fundamental Not END
 * @endcode
 *
 * Accepted labels are:
 *
 * - `Impossible`
 * - `Core`
 * - `Fundamental`
 * - `Not`
 * - `And`
 * - `Or`
 * - `Complex`
 *
 * These are repository serialization labels. In theory-facing prose they map to
 * the paper's category names.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_formulas Formula language
 *
 * A formula may contain:
 *
 * - feature variables,
 * - `TRUE` and `FALSE`,
 * - negation `!`,
 * - conjunction `&&`,
 * - disjunction `||`,
 * - implication `->`,
 * - equivalence `<->`,
 * - parentheses,
 * - `COUNT(...)` expressions.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_precedence Operator precedence
 *
 * The current parser uses this precedence order:
 *
 * 1. `!`
 * 2. `&&`
 * 3. `||`
 * 4. `->`
 * 5. `<->`
 *
 * Associativity in the current parser:
 *
 * - `->` is right-associative,
 * - `<->` is left-associative.
 *
 * @subsection spl_authoring_count COUNT(...) expressions
 *
 * `COUNT(...)` counts how many listed declared fundamental features are true in
 * a candidate system.
 *
 * Supported forms are:
 *
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) = k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) != k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) < k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) <= k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) > k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) >= k`
 * - `COUNT(f1, f2, ..., fn) IN [a,b]`
 *
 * Implementation rules checked against the parser:
 *
 * - every listed name must already appear in `FEATURES`,
 * - duplicate names inside one `COUNT(...)` list are rejected,
 * - `IN [a,b]` is inclusive,
 * - `a <= b` is required.
 *
 * `COUNT(...)` is repository surface syntax for the paper's cardinality idea.
 * The paper presents equivalent constraints arithmetically, for example as
 * `fx + fy + fz <= 1`.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_consistency Availability consistency rules
 *
 * The current implementation rejects a specification if:
 *
 * - an available system is not valid,
 * - a missing system is not valid,
 * - the same system is listed as both available and missing,
 * - both sections are present but do not form a disjoint partition of the valid
 *   system space.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_minimal Minimal complete example
 *
 * A minimal complete file with only the mandatory section:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL TwoFeatureUnconstrained
 * FEATURES 2 fx fy
 * @endcode
 *
 * This corresponds to the canonical fixture
 * `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/unconstrained_2f.spl`.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_full Full worked example
 *
 * A fuller file with constraints, missing systems, and a reporting filter:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * SPL TwoFeatureFxImpliesFyMissingS0
 *
 * FEATURES 2 fx fy
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT fx -> fy
 * END
 *
 * MISSING_SYSTEMS 1 END
 *
 * CATEGORY_FILTER Core Fundamental Not END
 * @endcode
 *
 * In this example:
 *
 * - the declared basis is `{fx, fy}`,
 * - only systems satisfying `fx -> fy` are valid,
 * - system `1` is marked as missing if it is valid,
 * - reporting is restricted to the selected categories.
 *
 * @section spl_authoring_afterparse What happens after parsing
 *
 * After parsing, the repository derives executable semantics from the file
 * through:
 *
 * - @ref feature_isolation::spl_specification_t "spl_specification_t"
 * - @ref feature_isolation::valid_system_ids
 * - @ref feature_isolation::available_valid_system_ids
 * - @ref feature_isolation::missing_valid_system_ids
 * - @ref feature_isolation::theory_system_space
 * - @ref feature_isolation::spl_processor_t "spl_processor_t"
 *
 * That system space is then consumed by feature analysis and isolation.
 */